Make platformio_project able to build and upload with a Bazel-provided
PlatformIO binary.

Upstream, the build and upload actions resolve `platformio` from a hard-coded
PATH (`/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin`), which forces the tool to
be installed out-of-band on the host (e.g. a symlink in /usr/local/bin pointing
at a virtualenv). This adds an optional `platformio` attribute; when set, the
build action invokes that executable instead, and the generated `bazel run`
upload script invokes it from the runfiles tree. This allows a hermetic,
Bazel-managed PlatformIO (see //starlark/platformio:platformio).

It also puts PlatformIO's tool-esptoolpy package directory on PYTHONPATH for the
build and upload commands. The rules_python console-script bootstrap that
launches the Bazel-managed PlatformIO exports PYTHONSAFEPATH=1, which PlatformIO
leaks into the esptool.py shim it runs for Espressif targets. That shim relies
on the implicit "script directory on sys.path" behavior to import its sibling
`esptool` package, so without this PYTHONPATH entry the build fails with
`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'esptool'`.

--- platformio/platformio.bzl
+++ platformio/platformio.bzl
@@ -66,6 +66,27 @@
 # Command that executed the PlatformIO system to upload data files to the
 # device's FS
 _FS_UPLOAD_COMMAND="platformio run -s -d {project_dir} -t uploadfs"
+
+
+# Shell setup that makes PlatformIO's bundled esptool importable when
+# PlatformIO is provided by Bazel (see the `platformio` attribute on
+# platformio_project).
+#
+# When PlatformIO is launched through rules_python's console-script bootstrap,
+# that bootstrap exports PYTHONSAFEPATH=1 so its own imports stay hermetic.
+# PlatformIO leaks the variable into the tool subprocesses it spawns, and the
+# Espressif `esptool.py` shim then fails with
+# `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'esptool'`, because it relies on Python
+# implicitly putting the script's own directory on sys.path so it can import
+# its sibling `esptool` package. PYTHONSAFEPATH does not strip PYTHONPATH
+# entries, so add PlatformIO's tool-esptoolpy package directory there instead.
+# The path mirrors how PlatformIO resolves its core directory. `cd ~` is used
+# rather than `$HOME` because HOME is unset inside the Bazel action sandbox.
+_ESPTOOL_PYTHONPATH_SETUP = (
+    "PIO_HOME=\"$(cd ~ && pwd)\"\n" +
+    "PIO_CORE=\"${PLATFORMIO_CORE_DIR:-$PIO_HOME/.platformio}\"\n" +
+    "export PYTHONPATH=\"$PIO_CORE/packages/tool-esptoolpy${PYTHONPATH:+:$PYTHONPATH}\""
+)
 
 
